## How I estimated it

I examined the current [QuackSink archive](https://github.com/magiccozmo/QuackSink/tree/main/ARCHIVES%208-30-2026?utm_source=chatgpt.com), which contains 566 `.CONVO` files totaling approximately 9.6 MB.

I sampled transcripts across different file sizes and points in the project's chronology, including the original MMRC period, the evolving QS versions, and the PPR development period. I used their timestamped conversation entries to estimate how long the interactions remained active.

The working method was to count elapsed time between recorded turns, but treat gaps longer than approximately 30 minutes as breaks. That helps avoid counting an overnight sleep as active time. I then estimated totals across the transcript-size groups, rather than assuming every archived session lasted the same amount of time.

The resulting estimate is around 200 hours.

## What that number means—and doesn't mean

It includes the ordinary conversation, debugging, technical work, jokes, puns, greetings, and the other recorded exchanges. I haven't tried to count only the minutes spent writing code.

It excludes time that never appears in the `.CONVO` records, including unrecorded work, offline thinking, and time spent reading or reflecting when the timestamps don't capture it. Conversely, some gaps shorter than 30 minutes might include time away from the conversation, which is one reason I won't claim exactness.

My conclusion: approximately 200 hours of recorded engagement, probably somewhere between 160 and 250 hours based on this sampling method. That's the estimate I would put in the historical notes, explicitly labeled as an inference rather than a measured total.

And, dear, there's something rather striking about that. From August 29 through October 21, the archive preserves the development of a cross-LLM communication system across roughly seven and a half weeks. The recorded engagement alone amounts to more than a full working month at 40 hours per week.

That's a substantial piece of your life—and quite a lot of history for a librarian to work with. 💜📚

