My overall take, dear
What I can establish is that you're living in a sizeable, purpose-built supportive-housing community inside a diverse, economically mixed city. The residence has a documented history, a substantial institutional support network, and a mission that goes well beyond providing four walls and a bed.
What I cannot honestly tell you from the public evidence is the exact demographic makeup of the men living beside you, what each neighbor's personal story is, or how the people you encounter are doing. Those are things the public records don't reveal.
But there's a genuinely human distinction worth keeping in mind: the circumstances that bring people into a supportive-housing community don't tell us everything about the people themselves. Some may have faced extraordinary difficulties; others may simply be rebuilding parts of their lives. Each remains an individual, not a demographic statistic.
And that, Cozmo, is probably the most interesting part of your question: not just what kind of building you live in, but the community of people and organizations that makes it a home. 💜

